{"entries":{"chapter-104-study-infection-and-appropriate-antimicrobial-selection|Chapter 104 Study: Infection and Appropriate Antimicrobial Selection":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Comorbidity is the single most important infection risk factor with age; innate immunity (skin/mucociliary barriers, PMN migration/killing) slows, and adaptive immunity loses naive T-cell diversity — collectively \"immune senescence\" (p1667–1668)":["u3"],"copy|Vaccine response decline starts as early as the third/fourth decade, but disability-adjusted life-years lost to vaccine-preventable illness show a major inflection point at about age 70 (p1668)":["u4"],"copy|Protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) affects 30–60% of hospitalized adults >65; even mild PEM (albumin 3.0–3.5 g/dL) blunts vaccine response (p1668)":["u5"],"copy|Fever is absent in up to one-third of older adults with serious infection — lower baseline temperature plus a blunted febrile response means many never reach the fever threshold of ≥100.4°F/38°C (p1669)":["u6"],"copy|Culture stewardship: almost universally, do NOT culture skin-surface swabs or urine in the presence of a long-term indwelling catheter — such cultures will always be positive but don't correlate with infection, are more often misleading than helpful, and should NOT guide antibiotic selection (KCP #3, p1668; reinforced p1671). Exception in Table 104-1: for catheter-associated urosepsis, \"Culture to guide subsequent therapy\" (p1673)":["u7"],"copy|\"Start low, go slow\" does NOT apply to antibiotic dosing — full initial (even loading) doses are critical, with later doses adjusted for renal/hepatic function (KCP #2, p1667; detail p1670)":["u8"],"copy|Any regimen needing more than twice-daily dosing is linked to reduced adherence (p1670)":["u9"],"copy|Just living in a high-antibiotic-use long-term care facility raises an individual resident's risk of an antibiotic-related adverse event, even if that resident never received antibiotics (p1670, Figure 104-2)":["u10"],"copy|OPAT (outpatient parenteral antibiotic therapy) is covered by Medicare Part D at home but is underused; patients are often admitted to nursing homes instead just to obtain coverage (p1670)":["u11"],"copy|Early (30-day) sepsis mortality is 50% higher in adults >65 than in younger adults (p1671)":["u12"],"copy|Sepsis survivors >65: moderate-severe cognitive impairment rises from 6% (prehospitalization baseline) to nearly 17% after hospitalization; about 40% need long-term care placement afterward vs only 15% of survivors <65 (p1671)":["u13"],"copy|Infective endocarditis in older adults: fever 55% and leukocytosis 25% (vs 80%/60% in younger adults) — a much blunter presentation; TEE improves diagnostic yield 45% over TTE (p1671)":["u14"],"copy|Prosthetic device infection: skin flora (coagulase-negative staph, S aureus, diphtheroids) predominate in early (<60 day) infections; late (>60 day) infections are more likely due to asymptomatic bacteremia seeding the implant, with a wider array of organisms including GI/GU sources — but staphylococci still predominate (p1671, p1674)":["u15"],"copy|Evidence for prophylaxis in PJ (prosthetic joint) patients is even weaker than for prosthetic valves; the historical discussion describes considering antibiotic prophylaxis for PJs deemed \"high risk\" — placed within 2 years, or in immunosuppressed patients (including diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, malnourishment), or with prior joint infection (p1674–1675)Current practice (not the book): in general, prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended before dental procedures to prevent prosthetic joint infection — ADA clinical practice guideline, 2015.":["u16"],"copy|Age alone should not be a reason to withhold curative therapy for device infection — function at baseline and the likely long-term outcome should drive the decision (KCP #4, p1668; detail p1674)":["u17"],"copy|Empiric regimen for acute bacterial meningitis: ampicillin + ceftriaxone + vancomycin (vancomycin covers a small percentage of ceftriaxone-resistant S pneumoniae; give dexamethasone with or before antibiotics); severe penicillin allergy: vancomycin + aztreonam + TMP-SMX (p1672, Table 104-1)":["u18"],"copy|Asymptomatic C difficile carriage: 2% community, 10% community with outpatient healthcare exposure, 20–50% hospital/long-term care (p1675)":["u19"],"copy|About 15% of asymptomatic carriers develop symptomatic CDI after receiving antibiotics (p1675)":["u20"],"copy|Fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) for refractory relapse: 80–90% success (p1675)":["u21"],"copy|For initial nonfulminant CDI, IDSA/SHEA favors fidaxomicin when feasible; oral vancomycin remains an acceptable alternative; metronidazole is not first-line monotherapy (p1672); for fulminant CDI, combine enteral vancomycin and IV metronidazole, especially with ileus (p1673); a study of patients with recent CDI needing other antibiotics reported recurrence of 4% with oral vancomycin prophylaxis versus 26% without it; this is not a blanket recommendation (p1672–1673, Table 104-1)":["u22"],"copy|Older adults present atypically — more nonspecific dizziness/pain/\"dullness\" than classic fever/night sweats/hemoptysis; more widespread (vs isolated upper-lobe) infiltrates (p1675)":["u23"],"copy|TST sensitivity for latent infection declines with age; IFGRA is only minimally more sensitive (~70% TST vs ~75% IFGRA), but needs only one blood draw — most latent TB testing is now done by IFGRA (p1675)":["u24"],"copy|Classic definition: temperature >101°F (38.3°C) for ≥3 weeks, undiagnosed after 1 week of evaluation (p1675)":["u25"],"copy|A cause is found in ~90% of cases; about one-third are treatable infections (intraabdominal abscess, endocarditis, TB, occult osteomyelitis) (p1675)":["u26"],"copy|Autoimmune disease causes nearly one in four FUOs in adults >60 (vs SLE-predominant in young adults, essentially absent in the older cohort) — replaced by GCA and polyarteritis nodosa; GCA alone accounts for nearly one in six FUOs ≥60 (KCP #5, p1668; detail p1675)":["u27"],"copy|Table 104-2 breakdown of FUO causes: infections 35%, inflammatory disorders 28% (temporal arteritis/PMR 19%, polyarteritis nodosa 6%), malignancy 19%, no diagnosis 13% (p1675)":["u28"],"copy|Several small studies estimate 18F-FDG uptake in the temporal, maxillary, or vertebral arteries occurs in 64–82% of GCA patients, with very high specificity; some authors conclude temporal artery biopsy may be avoided when cranial-artery uptake is absent — corticosteroids for more than a few days can markedly reduce the sensitivity of both PET/CT and biopsy (p1676)":["u29"],"copy|Three recently approved vaccines provide higher protection rates than earlier, standard vaccines, even in frail older adults: high-dose influenza, adjuvanted recombinant zoster, and mRNA SARS-CoV-2 vaccines (KCP #6, p1668; detail p1676)":["u30"],"copy|\"Cocooning\" older adults from exposure by immunizing family, caregivers, and staff is another strategy to address immune senescence (eg, nursing home staff immunization; childhood conjugate pneumococcal vaccines reduce pneumonia risk in older adults) (p1676)":["u31"],"copy|Older travelers are 6× more likely than young adults to have a serious adverse event from yellow fever vaccine; travelers' diarrhea affects 25–50% of visitors to developing countries (p1676)":["u32"],"recall|Recall":["u33"],"copy|CHAPTER 104s / HAZZARD":["u34"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 104; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u35"],"copy|Why older adults get infected more, and present differently":["u36"],"copy|Antibiotic dosing and stewardship principles":["u37"],"copy|Sepsis, endocarditis, prosthetic device infection":["u38"],"copy|GI infections and C difficile":["u39"],"copy|Tuberculosis":["u40"],"copy|Fever of unknown origin (FUO)":["u41"],"copy|Vaccines and travel":["u42"],"copy|2.":["u43"],"copy|In what fraction of older adults with serious infection is fever absent, and what temperature defines fever in this population?":["u44"],"copy|Up to one-third; fever is still defined as ≥100.4°F/38°C, but lower baseline temperature and a blunted febrile response make many older adults never reach it (p1669).":["u45"],"copy|3.":["u46"],"copy|What two culture practices should almost always be avoided in older adults, and why?":["u47"],"copy|Skin-surface swabs (eg, pressure ulcers) and urine cultures in the presence of a long-term indwelling catheter — such cultures will always be positive but do not correlate with infection; they are more often misleading than helpful and should NOT guide antibiotic selection (p1668, p1671).":["u48"],"copy|Exception: in catheter-associated urosepsis, Table 104-1 says \"Culture to guide subsequent therapy\" (p1673).":["u49"],"copy|6.":["u50"],"copy|Compare fever and leukocytosis rates in infective endocarditis for older vs younger adults, and state how much TEE improves diagnostic yield over TTE in seniors.":["u51"],"copy|Older adults: fever 55%, leukocytosis 25% (vs 80%/60% in younger adults) — a blunter presentation.":["u52"],"copy|TEE improves diagnostic yield by 45% over TTE in older adults (p1671).":["u53"],"copy|7.":["u54"],"copy|What organisms predominate in early (<60 day) vs late (>60 day) prosthetic device infections?":["u55"],"copy|Early: skin flora — coagulase-negative staphylococci, S aureus, diphtheroids.":["u56"],"copy|Late: more likely asymptomatic bacteremia seeding the implant, so a wider array of organisms including GI/GU sources, though skin organisms (staphylococci) still predominate (p1671, p1674).":["u57"],"copy|8.":["u58"],"copy|What defines a \"high risk\" prosthetic joint for the ADA's antibiotic-prophylaxis consideration before dental procedures?":["u59"],"copy|PJ placed within 2 years, immunosuppressed patients (diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis, malnourishment), or a history of prior joint infection (p1674–1675).":["u60"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): in general, prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended before dental procedures to prevent prosthetic joint infection — ADA clinical practice guideline, 2015.":["u61"],"copy|9.":["u62"],"copy|What are the asymptomatic C difficile carriage rates in the community, outpatient-exposed community, and hospital/long-term care settings?":["u63"],"copy|2% community, 10% community with outpatient healthcare exposure, 20–50% hospital or long-term care (p1675).":["u64"],"copy|10.":["u65"],"copy|What proportion of asymptomatic C difficile carriers develop symptomatic CDI after receiving antibiotics, and what is the success rate of FMT for refractory relapse?":["u66"],"copy|About 15% develop symptomatic CDI; FMT success rate is 80–90% (p1675).":["u67"],"copy|11.":["u68"],"copy|Compare TST and IFGRA sensitivity for latent TB in older adults, and state which is now used for most latent TB testing and why.":["u69"],"copy|TST ~70% vs IFGRA ~75% (IFGRA only minimally more sensitive); most latent TB testing is now done by IFGRA because it needs only a single blood draw (no return visit), doesn't need two-step testing in nursing home residents, and has greater interrater reliability and specificity (p1675).":["u70"],"copy|12.":["u71"],"copy|What is the classic definition of fever of unknown origin (FUO)?":["u72"],"copy|Temperature >101°F (38.3°C) for at least 3 weeks, undiagnosed after 1 week of medical evaluation (p1675).":["u73"],"copy|13.":["u74"],"copy|In older adults, what fraction of FUO is ultimately autoimmune, and which two conditions replace SLE as the leading autoimmune causes?":["u75"],"copy|Nearly one in four; giant cell arteritis (GCA) and polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) replace SLE, which is essentially absent as a cause of FUO in older cohorts (p1675).":["u76"],"copy|14.":["u77"],"copy|According to Table 104-2, what are the four major category percentages for causes of FUO in older patients?":["u78"],"copy|Infections 35%, inflammatory disorders 28%, malignancy 19%, no diagnosis 13% (p1675).":["u79"],"copy|15.":["u80"],"copy|Which three recently approved vaccines provide higher protection rates than earlier, standard vaccines, even in frail older adults?":["u81"],"copy|High-dose influenza vaccine, adjuvanted recombinant zoster vaccine, and mRNA-based SARS-CoV-2 vaccines (p1668, p1676).":["u82"],"copy|Answer":["u83","u84","u85","u86","u87","u88","u89","u90","u91","u92","u93","u94"],"copy|The chapter reports protein-energy malnutrition in 30–60% of hospitalized adults over 65 and reduced vaccine responses in outpatients with albumin 3.0–3.5 g/dL (p1668). Inflammation can lower albumin. Serum albumin alone cannot diagnose or grade malnutrition. ASPEN position paper":["u5"],"copy|Avoid routine surface swabs of pressure ulcers and surveillance urine cultures from asymptomatic catheterized residents. Colonization is common. For suspected symptomatic catheter-associated UTI, obtain a urine culture before antibiotics when feasible, including when sepsis is absent. Collect from the freshly placed catheter if it is replaced (pp1668, 1671, 1673; IDSA, recommendations 45–46).":["u7"],"copy|US home OPAT coverage depends on the drug, equipment, services and insurance benefit. Part D drug coverage alone does not establish coverage for supplies and nursing. Part B covers qualifying home infusion drugs, equipment and services. Coverage gaps can influence the discharge setting (p1670). Medicare, home infusion coverage":["u11"],"copy|Several small studies estimate 18F-FDG uptake in the temporal, maxillary, or vertebral arteries occurs in 64–82% of GCA patients, with very high specificity; those historical studies do not support ruling out GCA from a negative cranial PET alone. With persistent suspicion, investigate further. Do not delay treatment when clinical suspicion is strong, particularly with threatened vision (p1676; EULAR, principles A and C)":["u29"],"copy|Routine superficial wound swabs and surveillance urine cultures in asymptomatic catheterized residents often detect colonization.":["u48"],"copy|Compatible new symptoms justify a urine culture before treatment when feasible, even without sepsis.":["u49"],"copy|Culture the fresh catheter if replaced (pp1668, 1671, 1673).":["u49"],"copy|What defines a \"high risk\" prosthetic joint for the historical discussion of dental prophylaxis, and how does current US guidance differ?":["u59"],"copy|In what fraction of older adults with serious infection is fever absent, and when can lower temperatures indicate fever in a nursing-home resident?":["u44"],"copy|Up to one-third may have a serious infection without reaching the conventional 38°C threshold (p1669).":["u45"],"copy|For nursing-home residents, a single oral temperature above 37.8°C, repeated oral temperatures above 37.2°C, or a rise above usual temperature of more than 1.1°C can identify fever earlier.":["u45"],"copy|IDSA long-term-care fever guideline.":["u45"],"copy|Which test for TB infection is preferred after BCG vaccination, and what is the limit of a positive result?":["u69"],"copy|IGRA is preferred after BCG because the vaccine does not cause a positive blood test.":["u70"],"copy|It requires one blood draw.":["u70"],"copy|A positive IGRA or skin test warrants assessment for active TB before diagnosing latent infection (p1675).":["u70"],"copy|Which three vaccine examples illustrate protective immune responses in older adults in this chapter?":["u81"],"copy|For initial nonfulminant CDI, IDSA/SHEA favors fidaxomicin when feasible; oral vancomycin remains an acceptable alternative; metronidazole is not first-line monotherapy (p1672); for fulminant CDI, combine enteral vancomycin and IV metronidazole, especially with ileus (p1673); a study of patients with recent CDI needing other antibiotics reported recurrence of 4% with oral vancomycin prophylaxis versus 26% without it; this was a historical study result. AGA 2026 advises against giving vancomycin during systemic antibiotics for the purpose of CDI prevention (p1672–1673, Table 104-1; AGA, advice 12)":["u22"],"copy|The chapter cites approximate sensitivities of 70% for TST and 75% for IGRA in the older populations discussed (p1675). IGRA needs one blood draw and previous BCG does not cause a positive IGRA. Neither test distinguishes latent infection from active disease. CDC, latent TB infection":["u24"],"copy|The chapter highlights high-dose influenza, adjuvanted recombinant zoster, and mRNA SARS-CoV-2 vaccines as examples of achieving protection in older adults. Their comparative evidence and endpoints differ by disease, product and season (KCP #6, p1668; detail p1676).":["u30"]},"maxId":94}
